{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "**A Whirlwind in Japan: Temples, Treats, and Trains**\n\nLast month, I spent a week in Japan, and every moment felt like stepping into a postcard. My journey began in Tokyo, where neon lights and skyscrapers gave way to serene shrines like **Senso-ji** in Asakusa. The air smelled of fresh green tea and roasted nuts, and I lost myself in the maze-like alleys of **Ameya-Yokocho**, hunting for tiny, perfect souvenirs.\n\nFrom the chaos of the city, I boarded the **Shinkansen** to Kyoto. The bullet train glided effortlessly through countryside rice fields, arriving with the precision of clockwork. Kyoto’s ancient streets felt like a living museum. At **Fushimi Inari**, thousands of red torii gates stretched up a misty mountain, each one a wish left behind. I climbed until the path vanished, surrounded only by stone foxes and the hum of cicadas.\n\nFood became its own adventure. In Kyoto, I sipped **matcha** at a teahouse with seasoned geishas, their movements graceful as brushed ink. Later, in Osaka, I devoured **takoyaki**—crispy, savory octopus balls—from a street cart, laughing as sauce dripped down my fingers.\n\nMy last stop was Hiroshima. The **Peace Memorial Park** stood quiet but powerful, a reminder of resilience. Nearby, the **Miyajima** floating torii gate at sunset was pure magic—water lapping at its base as lanterns flickered to life.\n\nJapan left me breathless. It’s a place where tradition dances with futuristic tech, where every corner holds wonder. Already, I’m plotting my return.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.06550939381122589}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -0.30262133479118347}, {"token": " Wh", "logprob": -0.04418794438242912}, {"token": "irl", "logprob": -0.012623289600014687}, {"token": "wind", "logprob": -7.652943895664066e-05}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.112654685974121}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.